      "text": "I want to be straightforward with you here, because I think it's important for your academic work.\n\n**The Lydia Davis translation of *Madame Bovary* (Penguin Classics/Viking, 2010) is a commercially published, in-copyright work.** I'm not going to attempt to retrieve or reproduce substantial passages from it — doing so would raise serious copyright concerns, and it wouldn't be the right thing to do regardless of the deadline pressure.\n\nHere's what I'd genuinely recommend for getting the text you need **tonight or tomorrow morning**:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Legitimate Ways to Access the Text Quickly\n\n1. **Your university library's digital resources** — This is your best bet. Most university libraries have:\n   - **eBook editions** of *Madame Bovary* accessible instantly with your student login (check your library catalog for ProQuest Ebook Central, EBSCO, or similar platforms)\n   - **Overdrive/Libby** digital lending, which may have the Davis translation specifically\n\n2. **Ask a classmate** — Someone in your French lit class almost certainly has a copy you could borrow for a couple of hours.\n\n3. **Your professor or department** — Email your professor tonight explaining the situation. They may have a PDF of the relevant excerpt they can share for class use, or can point you to a course reserve copy.\n\n4. **Free public-domain translations** — The Lydia Davis translation is the one assigned, but for the purposes of *identifying and analyzing the structural pattern* of the agricultural fair scene, an older public-domain translation will show you exactly the same intercutting technique. You can access:\n   - The **Eleanor Marx Aveling translation** freely on [Project Gutenberg](https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2413) — Part II, Chapter 8\n   - The **original French** on [Wikisource](https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Madame_Bovary) — especially valuable for a French lit class, since FID (*style indirect libre*) is even more visible in Flaubert's original prose\n\n5. **Google Books / HathiTrust** — The Davis translation may have limited preview pages available that include the passage you need.\n\n---\n\nI know that's not the instant answer you were hoping for given your timeline, but those routes should get you to the actual text quickly and legitimately. In the meantime, the structural and analytical breakdown I gave you in my previous response covers the scene's architecture in detail — that should be genuinely useful as a framework while you track down the exact wording. Good luck tomorrow! 📖",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
